2026-10-27 — v0.5 FUTURE-DETECTOR / LARGER-COUPLING GATE
========================================================

WHY THIS STAGE EXISTS
---------------------
v0.4 showed that the validated BSM propagation effect is real but invisible
in the 641-day IceCube contained-event response after profiling astrophysical
normalization and spectral tilt:
  strongest tested charge-radius point (-6e-32 cm^2):
  Z_profile_norm_tilt ~ 0.02.

v0.5 therefore asks two separate questions:
  1. Can a larger coupling that is still compatible with an external bound
     move the distortion into higher-statistics energy bins?
  2. Would IceCube-Gen2 or a substantially larger tau-neutrino telescope then
     achieve >=2 sigma in a realistic exposure?

EXTERNAL TAU CHARGE-RADIUS BOUND
--------------------------------
Atzori Corona et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 135, 231803 (2025),
arXiv:2504.05272, report the global-fit 1-sigma interval
  -20 <= <r_nu_tau^2> / (1e-32 cm^2) <= +20.
v0.5 therefore expands the charge-radius scan to +/-20e-32 cm^2.

IMPORTANT: that global analysis also emphasizes momentum dependence of the
effective neutrino charge radius.  The constant high-Q charge-radius benchmark
used here is therefore a controlled phenomenological benchmark, not a claim
that an arbitrary low-Q fitted radius must remain constant through PeV DIS.

FORWARD-BLIND DERIVATIVE SCAN
-----------------------------
eps_em_inf is also extended to +/-0.1, +/-0.2, +/-0.3 and +/-0.5.
These points are explicitly labelled EXPLORATORY.  v0.5 does not claim a
model-independent laboratory allowed range for eps_em_inf.  Their purpose is
to determine how large a genuinely forward-blind high-Q interaction would
have to be for future telescopes to see it.

ICECUBE-GEN2 PROJECTION
-----------------------
No invented Gen2 response matrix is used.

Public Gen2 design information states:
  * optical-array sensitivity improves by about a factor of five;
  * IceCube-Gen2 will detect about 5-10 times as many neutrinos above 30 TeV;
  * the optical instrumented volume is about 8 km^3;
  * identified tau-neutrino rate above 300 TeV is expected to reach >=1/year,
    compared with about 0.2/year in IceCube.

v0.5 therefore carries two transparent 10-year scale scenarios:
  baseline:   Aeff x5
  optimistic: Aeff x10
and separately quotes the x5 tau-tag-rate scaling.

Because multiplying every response bin by a common exposure/area factor leaves
the profiled best-fit nuisance parameters unchanged, the profiled Asimov
significance scales exactly as sqrt(area x exposure).  This is tested in the
unit suite.

GENERIC FUTURE TAU TELESCOPE
----------------------------
Rather than attaching unsupported effective areas to GRAND/Trinity/TAMBO,
v0.5 also reports the detector-independent acceptance multiplier required for
2 sigma and 3 sigma in 10 years, and tabulates x10, x30, x100, x300, x1000.

ENERGY LOCALIZATION
-------------------
The profiled likelihood is decomposed into:
  25-100 TeV
  100-300 TeV
  300-1000 TeV
  1-1.4 PeV

This determines whether the useful BSM information has actually migrated into
a populated optical-telescope band or remains confined to nearly opaque PeV
bins better matched to Earth-skimming UHE tau concepts.

DECISION RULE
-------------
Only charge-radius points inside the quoted 2025 external 1-sigma interval
are allowed to trigger the "externally bounded" publishability decision.
Derivative-em points are reported separately and never promoted to "allowed"
solely because current IceCube lacks sensitivity.

