v0.8 IceCube parser failure: the official response is headerless and reports
area in m^2. Treating the first numerical line as a header and failing to
convert m^2 to cm^2 produced 0.001767 rather than ~17.6746 SM events.
The attempted in-place conversion also failed because NumPy supplied a
read-only array. The corrected copy*1e4 produced 17.674595. KM3NeT's
published Ehlert bright-track benchmark is 0.242767/335 d; the v0.8
calculation gave 0.242754, and central-90% 0.211081 vs 0.211117.

Analytic identities: for a common flux amplitude kappa, N_I=kappa I and
N_K=kappa K, so a limit kappa<=U/I implies N_K<=U K/I. If a common
interaction multiplies both detector acceptances similarly, the ratio K/I
changes little even if each cross section grows. For Case 6 specifically,
its NC final state does not create the charged muon required by the published
KM3NeT bright-track selection. The old K*(1+r) column is not a physical
bright-track prediction. A tau-only interaction also cannot multiply the
entire all-flavor IceCube effective area by 1+r; only the tau component is
eligible even before acceptance/propagation corrections.

IceCube's published 12.6-y EHE likelihood is a binned Poisson product in
reconstructed energy and direction, with an astrophysical nuisance flux and
pseudoexperiment calibration. The public DOI:10.7910/DVN/JHK49D release has
only total/per-flavor true-energy effective areas, a 10-point observed
differential-limit table and a demo; it does not supply the binned observed
counts, migration matrices, background templates or nuisance priors needed
to evaluate the exact likelihood for a new cross section. We will not call
an effective-area fold the published likelihood or claim an exact 90% CL.

v0.9 controlled public-data calculation: compare a model's exposure-weighted
true-energy yield in each published sliding one-decade E^-1 window to the
yield obtained from that window's reported 90%-CL differential limit. This
is an energy-dependent constraint proxy; overlapping windows and the changed
BSM response prevent a global coverage claim. Report both all-flavor and
tau-only upper-response proxies, and the unchanged direct track rate.
Numerical question: reproduce the v0.8 IceCube and KM3 SM folds to 0.5%,
then measure window ratios and the implied limiting common-flux scale for
the four benchmark cutoffs. Minimum calculation: read frozen CSV/JSON and
v0.8 cross-section table; one-dimensional quadrature only. Pass criteria:
unit-conversion assertion, sum-of-flavors area check, KM benchmark within
0.5%, no positive direct Case-6 muon-track increment, and finite positive
window ratios. No LHAPDF recomputation is needed; 14-core parallelism would
oversubscribe this small post-processing task.

